func=jag::game::ApplyLocChange	func_addr=0x00136fc0	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::game::ApplyLocChange -- the single chokepoint every server-driven loc mutation passes through.\nSupersede-then-append into the pending loc-change list at locChangeManager +0x60 (begin) / +0x68\n(end) / +0x70 (capacity), whose elements are 16-byte { refcount holder, loc record } pairs.\n\nlocChangeRef is one such pair: locChangeRef[0] \e refcount holder, locChangeRef[1] \e loc record.\nRecord fields read here (consistent with the engine APPLY_LOC_CHANGE note): plane  (low 2 bits\nused), tileX , tileY , shape , discriminator .\n\nTwo records collide when this key matches:\n    tileX << 14 | tileY | (plane & 3) << 28 | shapeLayer | (shape - 0x17 > 1) << 32\nwith shapeLayer derived from the shape byte by the standard RS loc-layer split:\n    shape < 4  or shape \e\e 0x17 -> 0x00000000   (wall)\n    shape < 9  or shape \e\e 0x18 -> 0x40000000   (wall decoration)\n    shape <\e 0x15               -> 0x80000000   (scenery)\n    shape >  0x15               -> 0xC0000000   (ground decoration)\nOn the first collision the routine switches to an erase-remove compaction that drops (DecRef) every\nfurther colliding entry, then unconditionally falls through to push_back of locChangeRef with an\nIncRef, growing via 0x00136210 when at capacity.\n\nEvidence: (1) callers are already-identified loc packet handlers -- jag::PacketHandlers::LocAddChange\n(0x00139495) and jag::PacketHandlers::LocDel (three sites), plus three more loc handlers at\n0x0013a260 / 0x0013ade0 / 0x00153de0; LocAddChange builds the record then calls this with the\nmanager taken off the Client; (2) the shape-to-layer thresholds are the RS loc-shape layer mapping\nverbatim, and the coordinate pack is the standard tile key; (3) the engine APPLY_LOC_CHANGE hook is\nlive against this address and its recorded field layout (plane 0x8 / tileX 0xc / tileY 0x10 /\nshape 0x14) matches the reads here.\n\nNAME PROVENANCE: ApplyLocChange is this project's coinage, not a Jagex symbol -- it does not appear\nin the fully-symbolised reference build. Note it does not mutate the scene: it queues, superseding\nany pending change on the same tile and layer. Kept verbatim because the offset auto-updater\nrelocates by this string.\n\nUNVERIFIED: the engine note glosses the  discriminator as 1\eadd / 3\edel / 2\eanim, but the\ncollision test here requires the incoming record to be 4 and the existing one to be 2. Those\nvalues were not chased down; do not rely on that gloss.
