func=jag::MainLogicManager::KeyboardInputRecorder::OnKeyDown	func_addr=0x007663c0	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=⛔ TRAP: the keyboard rings use the OPPOSITE cursor convention from the mouse rings.\n\nThis producer indexes and advances the SECOND dword after the slot array, and bumps the FIRST one when the\ntwo collide (drop-oldest). So for the keyboard rings: first \e READ, second \e WRITE. Both mouse rings are the\nother way round - first \e WRITE, second \e READ - which was verified independently in SendEventMouseClick,\nPopEventMouseMoveSample and the movement producer. Anything writing these rings must not assume one\nconvention from the other.\n\nRecord layout confirmed here by the stores: keyId, charCode, rawKeyCode, repeat (byte), modifierMask,\ntimestamp. The timestamp is loaded from the same global monotonic clock the mouse movement producer uses,\nwhich independently corroborates that global.\n\nModifier folding: left/right variants of each modifier collapse onto one bit before being OR-ed into both the\nlive and the sticky mask. The repeat flag is set only when the key was already held AND the modifier mask is\nunchanged AND it is the same key as last time; it is a send filter only and never reaches the wire.
