func=jag::MainLogicManager::Construct	func_addr=0x0007af0c	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::MainLogicManager::Construct -- constructs the MainLogicManager that the Client holds a\npointer to, and, critically, WIRES UP BOTH INPUT RECORDERS.\n\nIt contains two sub-objects, each of which stores the InputHandler pointer (Input's global\nhandler) in its own field 0 and then registers eastl::function delegates on that handler:\n\n  KeyboardInputRecorder (first sub-object): three delegates -- key down, key up, key char.\n  Layout it initialises: the per-key held-flag array, the held/distinct counters, a 255-record\n  key-PRESS ring with its cursor pair, then a 255-record typed-character ring with its cursor\n  pair. The character ring's cursor pair ends exactly where the mouse recorder begins, which is\n  the independent confirmation of both ring extents.\n\n  MouseInputRecorder (second sub-object): eight delegates in this registration order --\n  button0 down, button0 up, button1 down, button1 up, button2 down, button2 up, MOUSE MOVE,\n  wheel. The seventh lands in the handler's onMouseMove list, which is the same list\n  jag::input::Input::OnMouseMotion dispatches; that is the proof that movement samples are\n  produced per OS event. Layout it initialises: a 50-record press ring + cursor pair, then a\n  200-record movement ring + cursor pair, each ring loop followed by an 8-byte store clearing\n  exactly that ring's own cursors.\n\nEverything a synthetic-input producer needs to address lives in these two sub-objects.\n\nName provenance: matches the prior work on the Win32 build; the class name is this project's\nestablished coinage for the object the Client holds here.
