func=jag::ClientProt::SendEventMouseClick	func_addr=0x00182fb0	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=Cursor roles and coordinate order, read off this function's own instructions:\n\n* The dword at [ring base + 50*stride] is the WRITE cursor; the dword 4 bytes later is the READ cursor.\n  This function loads the READ cursor, compares it against the WRITE cursor, returns when equal (empty),\n  and indexes the slot array with the READ cursor. It stores to NEITHER cursor - the tick retires the\n  entry separately. The movement ring uses the identical convention (its pop loads the second dword,\n  compares against the first, and advances the second), and the movement producer writes at the first\n  and advances it, colliding-with-read -> advance read. So across both rings: first dword \e write,\n  second \e read.\n  \e> The engine's OMainLogicManager.CLICK_BUFFER_TAIL is really the WRITE cursor and CLICK_BUFFER_HEAD\n     is really the READ cursor. Injecting an entry therefore means writing the slot the READ cursor\n     already points at and bumping the WRITE cursor - not bumping the one named HEAD.\n\n* Coordinate order: the packet's 32-bit position word is built as (slot[+8] << 16) | slot[+4], and the\n  wire is little-endian, so slot[+4] is the field that leaves first. Cross-checked against the movement\n  producer, which stores the first float argument at slot[+4]. \e> slot[+4] is X and slot[+8] is Y, which\n  matches MouseSample and the Windows offset table, and contradicts the Linux table's OMouseClickEntry\n  (which had X and Y transposed).
func=jag::ClientProt::SendEventMouseClick	func_addr=0x00182fb0	kind=plate	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=Setting prototype: void SendEventMouseClick(MouseProtSenderBase * sender)
func=jag::PacketHandlers::PointlightExtendBelow	func_addr=0x001e2080	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=OFFICIAL_PROT_NAME\ePOINTLIGHT_EXTEND_BELOW direction\eSERVER opcode\e218 size\e3\n\nPayload: g1_sub value (0xf clears the override); g2 pointlight id (BE).\nSets light+0x156 with the override flag at light+0x157 on every scene light matching the id.\nEvidence: the light-gathering pass FUN_006d0150 computes the LOWER level bound from this pair --\n"hasOverride \e light+0x157; v \e hasOverride ? light+0x156 : lightType+0x1d; lowerLevel \e v ? 0 :\nlight->level" -- while the +0x154/+0x155 pair raises the UPPER bound to 3. Dropping the bottom of\nthe level range to 0 is EXTEND_BELOW.
