func=jag::game::Location::Location	func_addr=0x004c9dc0	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::game::Location::Location. Installs vftable 0x014258c0. sizeof(Location) \e 0x3b0\n(pool element stride 0x3d0 at 0x004c9fd4 / 0x00158cd4, object embedded at controlblock+0x20).\nVerified fields: 0x1a8 typeId (param 4), 0x18d isDeleted, 0x18e isHidden, 0x120..0x153 placement\nblob (0x120 shape, 0x121 rotation), 0xc0/0xc8 shared_ptr visibleType, 0xd0/0xd8 originalType,\n0x31c highlightIntensity, 0x324 highlightCategory, 0x32c showAsImportant.\nshared_ptr member layout is {controlBlock @X, object @X+8}.
func=jag::ScriptRunner::GetClientScriptState	func_addr=0x00316890	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::ScriptRunner::GetClientScriptState — returns ClientScriptState* (pcVar4+8). Per-state slot 0xC420. The +0x28/+0x40/+0x58 EASTL vectors are LOCAL-variable arrays (params/locals); ExecuteHookInner writes a hook's int/string/long args into them. A script's RETURN VALUES are NOT here — they sit on the inline operand stacks: int data +0x100 / SP +0x10a0, string data +0x10a8 / SP +0x8da8, long data +0x8db0 / SP +0xacf8 (each SP is the depth, immediately after its 1000-element data block). Backs CS2Executor.readResults().
func=jag::ClientProt::FlushPerTick	func_addr=0x001a1700	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=Per-tick prot pump. Order established for synthetic-input work:\n\n* Single code caller is the tick body (jag::ConnectionManager::LoginProtocolHandler, itself likely\n  misnamed). That body calls THIS at a lower address than it calls the per-tick input-processing pass\n  (the SendIfButtonD-named function, which reads the click ring and builds the keyboard views), so the\n  pump appears to run BEFORE local input processing. Address order, not proven control flow - block\n  reordering could invalidate it. Verify before relying on it for anything destructive.\n* FlushMouseProtSender is called TWICE here - Linux genuinely has two mouse prot senders (EVENT then\n  NATIVE), not compiler duplication. The native one has no producer on Linux, so it flushes nothing.\n* The EVENT_KEYBOARD packet is built INLINE in this function rather than in a callee: it walks the\n  per-tick key-event view (begin/end pointers, 0x20 stride), counts records whose filter byte is clear,\n  writes a BE u16 byte-count \e n*4, then per record one byte plus a 24-bit BE time delta against the\n  previously emitted key event.\n\nConsequence for the engine: FlushMouseProtSender IS separately callable on Linux, so the movement-history\nstage can be driven the way SendEventMouseClick already is - write ring entries, invoke it, restore. An\nearlier pass concluded no callable equivalent existed on Linux; that is superseded.
func=jag::ClientProt::FlushPerTick	func_addr=0x001a1700	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=98	cu_index_from_entry=32	text=Keyboard packet source — this is NOT the key ring.\n\nThe vector walked here lives on a per-tick input-event view object reached from the client, at begin/end\npointers 8 bytes apart. Stride is the KeyEvent size; the emit filter is the record's repeat byte being clear;\nthe wire carries a big-endian u16 byte-count of records*4, then per record the LOW BYTE of keyId followed by a\n24-bit big-endian delta against the ClientProt's last-key-event-time field, which is advanced per record and\nsaturates at the 24-bit maximum.\n\nThe per-tick input pass refills this vector from the key ring by COPYING records, so the vector is not a view\nonto live ring storage. That is what makes a synthetic press deliverable without side effects: point begin/end\nat engine-owned records for the duration of one flush and restore them afterwards, and the ring, the per-key\nheld/seen arrays, the modifier masks and the CS2 dispatch are never involved.\n\nAlso visible in this prologue: the two mouse senders are sub-objects of the ClientProt aggregate (the EVENT\none is flushed first, the NATIVE one second), and the client back-pointer sits before them.
func=jag::ClientProt::FlushPerTick	func_addr=0x001a1700	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=407	cu_index_from_entry=111	text=jag::ClientProt::EVENT_KEYBOARD build site (op81, varShort) inside SendMultiDisplayPackets.\nDrains the per-tick key-event ring buffer (vector at [+0x88..+0x90], 0x20-byte entries).\nWire: [u16 BE] byteLen \e nEvents*4; then nEvents x 4-byte record:\n [1] keycode (press/release encoded in value); [3] BE timeDelta ms since prev key event (cap 0xFFFFFF).
func=jag::ClientProt::FlushPerTick	func_addr=0x001a1700	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=1840	cu_index_from_entry=436	text=op106 EVENT_APPLET_FOCUS (ProtEntry &jag::ClientProt::EVENT_APPLET_FOCUS , size 1). Computes keyboard-focus state via SDL_GetKeyboardFocus() \e\e our window; if it differs from the cached focus state, emits a 1-byte boolean (the new focus state) on op106. Sent only on focus CHANGE. This is the genuine 1-byte applet-focus boolean (NOT op56, NOT op105).
func=jag::opcode::SendBugReport	func_addr=0x002d7f60	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=NAME UNPROVEN: op7 size 1 (descriptor 0x016844d0). Pops one CS2 int and writes it as a single byte; guard requires client state (+0x19b28) \e\e 0x17 and mode (+0x28) \e\e 0x1e on the manager at client+0x19400 (the create / name-suggestion manager, see CreateSuggestNameReply). Payload size matches BUG_REPORT but nothing identifies the byte's meaning. Do not treat the BUG_REPORT label as verified.
