func=jag::PacketHandlers::IfSetobject	func_addr=0x00187980	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=OFFICIAL_PROT_NAME\eIF_SETOBJECT direction\eSERVER opcode\e36 size\e10\n\nPayload: [0..1] g2 objId (BE), [2..5] g4 count (32-bit), [6..9] g4 componentId.\nTail-calls 0x00123d00, the 32-bit-count sibling of jag::InterfaceManager::ApplyIfSetObject\n(0x00123b10). The already-named IfSetobjectLong (0x00186ea0) calls the 0x00123b10 variant with an\nidentical argument layout -- (client, componentId, objId, 0xFFFF, count) -- differing only in that\nit assembles a 64-bit count from two g4 reads (R13<<32 | R9) where this handler passes a single\n32-bit count in R8D. IF_SETOBJECT is exactly IF_SETOBJECT_LONG with a 32-bit count.
func=jag::PacketHandlers::SetInteractionTarget	func_addr=0x00188b00	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=SCOPE NOTE (949-1): op\e2 writes ONLY the Client+0x19450 interaction MARKER manager via game::SetInteractionMarkerEntry. It does NOT touch any PathingEntity +0x1B4 / +0x228 / +0x218 (the face/interaction-target fields the engine reads). It is the on-target interaction indicator/marker, a separate subsystem from the local player's model-facing. (2nd arg passed to SetInteractionMarkerEntry here is literal 1, not 14.)
