func=jag::input::Input::OnKeyCharInner	func_addr=0x00cb2ee0	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=Signature confirmed from the instructions, not inferred from the sibling functions:\n\n  void OnKeyCharInner(Input *this, int32 charCode)\n\nRDI is `this` (a mutex sits at +0x18 and is taken before the listener walk). ESI is the second argument: it\nis copied to EBP and then stored as a DWORD into a stack slot whose address is handed to each listener as the\nsecond argument, so it is a 32-bit value passed by pointer to the callbacks, exactly as the key down/up inner\nfunctions do with their key code. Nothing else is read from it, and there is no third parameter.\n\nThe walk is the usual one: every named handler's listener vector first, then the global handler's, stopping\nearly if a listener returns non-zero (i.e. consumes the character).\n\nThis is the typed-character path. The engine hooks the key down/up inner functions for its recorder but never\nhooked this one, which is why typed text was invisible in recordings.
